\fs96 \cf2 Calling Python from Mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The Python ecosystem is full of useful libraries, so you shouldn't have to rewrite them in Mojo. Instead, you can simply import Python packages and call Python APIs from Mojo. The Python code runs in a standard Python interpreter (CPython), so your existing Python code doesn't need to change.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Specify your Python version\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo doesn't include a CPython interpreter\'97it uses the CPython interpreter provided by your environment's default Python version. So be sure you know which Python version you're using in each environment where your Mojo code will run.\
To ensure you get consistent results, we recommend you {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/pixi/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 use Pixi}} to manage your package dependency and virtual environment. In a Pixi project, you can specify the Python version like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf12 \cb13 pixi \cf14 add\cf12  \cf15 "python==3.11"\cf12 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Now, even if your operating system's default Python version is something else, your Pixi project (and the Mojo code inside) always uses Python 3.11.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf12 \cb13 pixi run python \cf18 --version\cf12 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf12 \cb13 Python 3.11.0\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Import a Python module in Mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 To import a Python module in Mojo, just call {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/python/python/Python/#import_module"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb19 \ul \ulc5 Python.import_module()}} with the module name. The following shows an example of importing the standard Python {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://numpy.org/"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 NumPy}} package:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf12 \cb20 \uc0\u55357 \u56613  Mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 from\cf12  std\cf5 .\cf12 python \cf5 import\cf12  Python\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf12  \cf14 main\cf5 ()\cf12  raises\cf5 :\cf12 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf12 \cb13     \cf6 # This is equivalent to Python's `import numpy as np`\cf12 \cb1 \
\cb13     np \cf5 =\cf12  Python\cf5 .\cf12 import_module\cf5 (\cf15 "numpy"\cf5 )\cf12 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf6 # Now use numpy as if writing in Python\cf12 \cb1 \
\cb13     array \cf5 =\cf12  np\cf5 .\cf12 array\cf5 (\cf12 Python\cf5 .\cf14 list\cf5 (\cf18 1\cf5 ,\cf12  \cf18 2\cf5 ,\cf12  \cf18 3\cf5 ))\cf12 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf12 array\cf5 )\cf12 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Running this program produces the following output:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf12 \cb13 [1 2 3]\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Assuming that you have the NumPy package installed in your environment, this imports NumPy and you can use any of its features.\
If you want to use Python builtin APIs, you just need to import the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 builtins
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  module the same way. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf12 \cb20 \uc0\u55357 \u56613  Mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 from\cf12  std\cf5 .\cf12 python \cf5 import\cf12  Python\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf12  \cf14 main\cf5 ()\cf12  raises\cf5 :\cf12 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf12 \cb13     np \cf5 =\cf12  Python\cf5 .\cf12 import_module\cf5 (\cf15 "numpy"\cf5 )\cf12 \cb1 \
\cb13     array \cf5 =\cf12  np\cf5 .\cf12 array\cf5 (\cf12 Python\cf5 .\cf14 list\cf5 (\cf18 1\cf5 ,\cf12  \cf18 2\cf5 ,\cf12  \cf18 3\cf5 ))\cf12 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     builtins \cf5 =\cf12  Python\cf5 .\cf12 import_module\cf5 (\cf15 "builtins"\cf5 )\cf12 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf12 builtins\cf5 .\cf14 type\cf5 (\cf12 array\cf5 ))\cf12 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf12 \cb13 <class 'numpy.ndarray'>\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A few things to note:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 import_module()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method returns a reference to the module in the form of a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/python/python_object/PythonObject/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb19 \ul \ulc5 PythonObject}} wrapper. You must store the reference in a variable and then use it as shown in the example above to access functions, classes, and other objects defined by the module. See {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/python/types/#mojo-wrapper-objects"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Mojo wrapper objects}} for more information about the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 PythonObject
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  type.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Currently, you cannot import individual members (such as a single Python class or function). You must import the whole Python module and then access members through the module name.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Mojo doesn't yet support top-level code, so the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 import_module()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  call must be inside another method. This means you may need to import a module multiple times or pass around a reference to the module. This works the same way as Python: importing the module multiple times won't run the initialization logic more than once, so you don't pay any performance penalty.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
import_module()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  may raise an exception. Raising exceptions is much more common in Python code than in the Mojo standard library, which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/roadmap/#the-standard-library-has-limited-exceptions-use"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 limits their use for performance reasons}}.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
We recommend using a package manager such as pixi, uv, or conda to manage your environment. For instructions on setting up a Mojo project with pixi, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/get-started/#1-create-a-mojo-project"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Create a Mojo project}} in the Get started with Mojo tutorial.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf21 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf21 \cb22 Caution\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/cli/build/"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf2 \cb23 \ul \ulc2 mojo build}}\cf2 \cb22  doesn't include the Python packages used by your Mojo project. Instead, Mojo loads the Python interpreter and Python packages at runtime, so they must be provided in the environment where you run the Mojo program (such as inside the pixi environment where you built the executable).\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Import a local Python module\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 If you have some local Python code you want to use in Mojo, just add the directory to the Python path and then import the module.\
For example, suppose you have a Python file named 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb19 mypython.py
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf12 \cb20 \uc0\u55357 \u56333  mypython.py\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 import\cf12  numpy \cf5 as\cf12  np\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf12  \cf14 gen_random_values\cf5 (\cf12 size\cf5 ,\cf12  base\cf5 ):\cf12 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf12 \cb13     \cf6 # generate a size x size array of random numbers between base and base+1\cf12 \cb1 \
\cb13     random_array \cf5 =\cf12  np\cf5 .\cf12 random\cf5 .\cf12 rand\cf5 (\cf12 size\cf5 ,\cf12  size\cf5 )\cf12 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 return\cf12  random_array \cf5 +\cf12  base\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Here's how you can import it and use it in a Mojo file:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf12 \cb20 \uc0\u55357 \u56613  main.mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 from\cf12  std\cf5 .\cf12 python \cf5 import\cf12  Python\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf12  \cf14 main\cf5 ()\cf12  raises\cf5 :\cf12 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf12 \cb13     Python\cf5 .\cf12 add_to_path\cf5 (\cf15 "path/to/module"\cf5 )\cf12 \cb1 \
\cb13     mypython \cf5 =\cf12  Python\cf5 .\cf12 import_module\cf5 (\cf15 "mypython"\cf5 )\cf12 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     values \cf5 =\cf12  mypython\cf5 .\cf12 gen_random_values\cf5 (\cf18 2\cf5 ,\cf12  \cf18 3\cf5 )\cf12 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf12 values\cf5 )\cf12 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Both absolute and relative paths work with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/std/python/python/Python/#add_to_path"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb19 \ul \ulc5 add_to_path()}}. For example, you can import from the local directory like this:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28\fsmilli14400 \cf12 \cb20 \uc0\u55357 \u56613  Mojo\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf12 \cb13 Python\cf5 .\cf12 add_to_path\cf5 (\cf15 "."\cf5 )\cf12 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

